## T-169 — Format, Clippy, Cargo check each run to completion (post-fix)

Same run, 30460161440, "check" job breakdown:

    ✓ Format, lint, check in ~1m
      ✓ Set up job
      ✓ Run actions/checkout@v4
      ✓ Install Rust 1.91 toolchain
      ✓ Cache Cargo registry & build
      ✓ Format
      ✓ Clippy (workspace, all targets)
      ✓ Cargo check (workspace)

All three steps that were previously `skipped` (see baseline-03c.log
job breakdown) now execute and pass. This is the first real Actions
run in this project's history where the "Format, lint, check" job has
completed — confirmed via `gh run list`, which showed every prior run
of this job failing at the toolchain-install step back to baseline
commit 5de978d.

## T-170 — the gate fails on a real violation (not just passes on nothing)

Same pattern as Subject 03b's T-166: a passing gate on a clean tree
does not by itself prove the gate can catch anything. Scratch branch
`scratch/t170-lint-violation` added, to `crates/cli/src/main.rs`, a
deliberately non-compliant function (`clippy::bool_comparison` /
`clippy::needless_bool`, plus an unformatted body violating
`rustfmt`'s style), pushed, opened as PR #4 against `main`, deleted
after confirmation.

$ gh run view 30460920132
https://github.com/nabbisen/noye/actions/runs/30460920132
Conclusion: failure (job-level: "Format, lint, check" = failure; all
4 other jobs = success, confirming this is an isolated
format/lint-gate finding, not a general breakage)

Job log, step "Format":

    Diff in /home/runner/work/noye/noye/crates/cli/src/main.rs:95:
     // with nothing to catch. This branch is discarded after the CI run
     // confirms failure — never merge this function.
     fn t170_scratch_violation(x: bool) -> bool {
    -    if x==true { return true; } else { return false; }
    +    if x == true {
    +        return true;
    +    } else {
    +        return false;
    +    }
     }

     fn main() -> Result<()> {
    ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.

The job fails at the "Format" step, before Clippy or Cargo check even
run — the gate catches the violation at the first opportunity, as
expected. Proves the fixed toolchain-install step doesn't just let the
job run to a hollow "success": the later steps genuinely enforce
format and lint, and reject a real violation.

